#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# Composition, then hand-off. The window keeps the poll loop so the close
# button and the keyboard work; both edges run detached; the picture is a
# tree assembled here out of scene scripts that know nothing about each other.
#
#     ./bin/etcs scripts/streamed_scenes.etcs
#
# THE SHAPE, and how it differs from polygon_scene.etcs. That one ends on its
# detaches and drops you into the shell -- good for poking at a live runtime,
# but nothing is polling, so the window is inert. This one ends on main.Run(),
# which owns thread 1 for the window's lifetime: real input, a close button
# that closes, and the frame edge presenting the whole time. Closing the window
# raises the closure, the drain winds up both edges, and the Deletes below run
# against a runtime that has already stopped using what they delete.
#
#   1. this script's thread    main.Run()             poll loop (GLFW affinity)
#   2. a ThreadPool worker     Window.ProduceEvents   input ring -> stream
#   3. a detached thread       Window.ConsumeEvents   input stream drain
#   4. a ThreadPool worker     Surface.ProduceFrames  frame clock
#   5. a detached thread       Surface.ConsumeFrames  acquire, record, present
#
# HIGHER ORDER, and what that buys. scene_emblem.etcs is run TWICE, against two
# different anchors, and produces two emblems in two places from one
# description -- because every point in it is stated in its anchor's space and
# none of them is absolute. That is the Drawable contract's upward half doing
# work: a subtree is relocatable because its coordinates were never absolute.
#
# The scene scripts build structure and never draw. This file states the
# picture, once: one Clear to open a composition, one Draw of the stage to
# realise everything reachable from it. Splitting those two jobs is what lets
# a scene script be composed without knowing what else is on the surface.

spawn RenderProvider::Instance gpu
gpu.Create()

spawn WindowProvider::Window main
main.Create(1024, 768, 'ETCS composed scenes')

main.spawn(RenderProvider::Surface view)
view.Create(@gpu, modules/RenderProvider/shaders/)

# The stage: a rectangle the size of the frame, and the root of the tree.
# Ordinary in every way -- it is the polygon nobody nested inside anything.
spawn RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D stage
stage.Create()
stage.AddPoint(0, 0)
stage.AddPoint(1024, 0)
stage.AddPoint(1024, 768)
stage.AddPoint(0, 768)
stage.SetFill(0.06, 0.07, 0.11, 1.0)

# Two regions of the stage, in the stage's space. Each is a perfectly ordinary
# polygon that happens to be used as a mounting point.
stage.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D left)
left.Create()
left.AddPoint(60, 200)
left.AddPoint(460, 200)
left.AddPoint(460, 600)
left.AddPoint(60, 600)
left.SetFill(0.13, 0.15, 0.22, 1.0)
left.SetOrder(1)

stage.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D right)
right.Create()
right.AddPoint(560, 320)
right.AddPoint(960, 320)
right.AddPoint(960, 720)
right.AddPoint(560, 720)
right.SetFill(0.13, 0.15, 0.22, 1.0)
right.SetOrder(1)

# The same scene, twice, in two places. `run` waits, so the tree is fully
# built before the next line -- and each sub-script gets its own Root, its own
# signal context and its own closure, so both copies use the names `body` and
# `gem` without colliding.
run RenderProvider/scripts/scene_emblem.etcs anchor=left
run RenderProvider/scripts/scene_emblem.etcs anchor=right

# A different scene, mounted directly on the stage.
run RenderProvider/scripts/scene_bars.etcs anchor=stage

# The picture, stated once. Clear opens a composition; Draw appends the whole
# tree to it, depth-first in Order().
view.Clear(0.02, 0.02, 0.03, 1.0)
stage.Draw(@view)

# Hand off. Input first, then frames, then the poll loop.
detach WindowProvider/scripts/window_events.etcs window=main
detach RenderProvider/scripts/render_frames.etcs view=view

main.Run(1024, 768, 'ETCS composed scenes')

view.Delete()
gpu.Delete()
main.Delete()
